---
import { webmcpToolCount } from "@/lib/developers/webmcp-docs"
import HeadingAnchor from "@/components/heading-anchor"
import DevDocsLayout from "@/astro/layouts/DevDocsLayout.astro"

export const prerender = true
---

<DevDocsLayout
  title="Try NTUMods WebMCP in Chrome"
  description="How to test the NTUMods WebMCP tools today: turn on the Chrome flag, add a test extension, and ask for something on the timetable planner."
  path="/developers/webmcp/try"
  eyebrow="WebMCP"
  heading="Try it"
  intro="Turn on a Chrome flag, add a test extension, and ask for something on the timetable planner.">
  <div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <p>
      WebMCP is an early preview, so setup takes a few minutes and suits people
      comfortable installing a browser extension. If you would rather wait,
      nothing is lost: it will get easier as browsers and assistants catch up.
    </p>
    <h2 id="steps">
      Steps<HeadingAnchor id="steps" label="Steps" />
    </h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Use a recent Chrome.</strong> Chrome's origin trial starts from Chrome
        149, and these steps work for local testing.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turn WebMCP on.</strong> Open <code
          >chrome://flags/#enable-webmcp-testing</code
        > in the address bar, set the flag to Enabled, then relaunch Chrome.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add an assistant that can use website tools.</strong> Chrome does
        not include one that is documented to call WebMCP tools yet, so testers use
        an extension. Chrome's documentation lists two:
        <ul class="mt-2">
          <li>
            The <a
              href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/model-context-tool-inspec/gbpdfapgefenggkahomfgkhfehlcenpd"
              >Model Context Tool Inspector</a
            > from the Chrome Web Store. It shows the tools a page offers and lets
            you try them with prompts. It needs a Gemini API key.
          </li>
          <li>
            The <a href="https://github.com/GoogleChromeLabs/webmcp-extension/"
              >Example Agentic Chrome Extension</a
            > on GitHub. It needs a small local server and your own API key, so it
            suits developers more than most students.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open NTUMods and check the tools appear.</strong> Go to the <a
          href="/timetable">timetable planner</a
        > and open the extension. You should see about {webmcpToolCount} NTUMods tools
        listed. If you see none, check that the flag is enabled and that you relaunched
        Chrome.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ask for something specific</strong>, such as the <a
          href="/developers/webmcp/examples">examples</a
        >, and check the result on the page.
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      New to this? Run the <a
        href="https://googlechromelabs.github.io/webmcp-tools/demos/explainer/#compare"
        >comparison demo</a
      > with the same extension first. It is a small page with two tools, so it is
      an easy way to confirm your setup works before you try NTUMods.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cautions">
      Before you start<HeadingAnchor id="cautions" label="Before you start" />
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        An API key is tied to your own account and usage. Only paste it into an
        extension you trust, and keep an eye on any usage limits.
      </li>
      <li>
        Gemini in Chrome is a separate feature, and Chrome's documentation does
        not say it can call WebMCP tools.
      </li>
      <li>
        You can read the full list of NTUMods tools with no setup in <a
          href="/.well-known/webmcp.json">webmcp.json</a
        >, or on the <a href="/developers/webmcp/tools">tools page</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="read">
      What to read<HeadingAnchor id="read" label="What to read" />
    </h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp"
          >WebMCP on Chrome for Developers</a
        >: the overview, with setup and testing tools. Start here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://developer.chrome.com/docs/ai/webmcp/imperative-api"
          >Imperative API guide</a
        >: how a site registers tools, which is the approach NTUMods uses.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="https://github.com/webmachinelearning/webmcp"
          >WebMCP on GitHub</a
        > and the <a href="https://webmachinelearning.github.io/webmcp/"
          >specification</a
        >: the standard itself, where you can follow progress and report issues.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>
</DevDocsLayout>
